WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.614
Due to backwards compatibility C++ 
will never&nbsp;become any simpler.

00:00:04.614 --> 00:00:07.560
But the way that we use it can.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.798
Welcome back everyone. 
I'm James Murphy.

00:00:09.798 --> 00:00:13.682
And today&nbsp;we're talking about the 
simplest smart pointer in C++

00:00:13.682 --> 00:00:15.233
Unique Pointer `std::unique_ptr`

00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:16.819
This is a dumb pointer

00:00:16.819 --> 00:00:20.160
otherwise known as a raw 
pointer or just a pointer.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.175
But this is a smart pointer.

00:00:22.175 --> 00:00:24.213
So what makes it so&nbsp;smart?

00:00:24.213 --> 00:00:27.747
Smart pointers are just wrappers
 around regular old pointers

00:00:27.747 --> 00:00:30.502
that help you prevent very&nbsp;
common bugs.

00:00:30.502 --> 00:00:33.593
Namely, forgetting to delete a pointer 
and causing a memory leak

00:00:33.593 --> 00:00:37.176
or accidentally&nbsp;deleting a pointer 
twice or in the wrong way.

00:00:37.176 --> 00:00:39.848
They can be used in a similar 
way to normal&nbsp;pointers.

00:00:39.848 --> 00:00:43.941
And they automate away some of the manual 
process causing some of the most common&nbsp;bugs.

00:00:43.941 --> 00:00:46.680
Let's take a look at a 
specific example.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.575
We have a vector 
of pointers to widgets

00:00:48.575 --> 00:00:51.578
where each&nbsp;widget is allocated 
on the Heap using `new`.

00:00:51.578 --> 00:00:54.456
We're using a vector of pointers 
instead of a vector&nbsp;of actual widgets

00:00:54.456 --> 00:00:58.204
because we're imagining there might 
be some kind of inheritance hierarchy&nbsp;going on.

00:00:58.204 --> 00:01:01.463
In actuality, we just have 
a single widget class.

00:01:01.463 --> 00:01:05.630
It just stores an integer and prints&nbsp;
something on construction or destruction.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:10.609
Let's also suppose we've already written 
code using raw&nbsp;pointers or raw references.

00:01:10.609 --> 00:01:13.650
In this case we're just printing
 something from the widget.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.803
Getting back&nbsp;to our example. 
We have a pretty typical workflow.

00:01:16.803 --> 00:01:19.848
We create our vector and push 
five widgets onto it.

00:01:19.848 --> 00:01:22.169
Then we loop over them and 
do some work.

00:01:22.169 --> 00:01:25.576
Maybe the user closes a widget. 
So we pop that one off and&nbsp;delete it.

00:01:25.576 --> 00:01:30.686
Or maybe we peel some of the widgets off 
and do some different work with it.

00:01:30.686 --> 00:01:32.722
Once we're&nbsp;done with that 
one we delete it.

00:01:32.722 --> 00:01:36.117
At the end we loop over the vector and
 delete all the remaining&nbsp;widgets.

00:01:36.117 --> 00:01:39.902
This code works but it is 
very very error prone.

00:01:39.902 --> 00:01:43.048
Let's take a look at just a few of the ways&nbsp;
this could have gone wrong.

00:01:43.048 --> 00:01:44.552
First we're calling new in a loop.

00:01:44.552 --> 00:01:47.808
If the first new succeeds but 
the&nbsp;second one throws an exception,

00:01:47.808 --> 00:01:49.762
then we just leap the first one.

00:01:49.762 --> 00:01:51.558
To handle that case without&nbsp;
smart pointers,

00:01:51.558 --> 00:01:55.608
you'd need something like 
a try-except and or go to fail.

00:01:55.608 --> 00:01:58.980
No real issues just&nbsp;looping through 
and using the widgets though.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:03.720
But in this case, say a widget is closed,&nbsp;
it's very easy to forget to delete it.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.494
This is one of the most common 
ways that memory&nbsp;is leaked.

00:02:06.494 --> 00:02:10.428
Most likely nothing will go wrong with
 your program if you forget to delete this pointer.

00:02:10.428 --> 00:02:13.382
You've just got a very very 
tiny memory leak.

00:02:13.382 --> 00:02:17.982
This case is even easier to forget because 
the delete&nbsp;has to be after the use.

00:02:17.982 --> 00:02:22.551
And of course, if all the widgets are closed, it's 
very easy to forget to&nbsp;delete all of them.

00:02:22.551 --> 00:02:26.356
Or instead of forgetting to delete the widget, 
you might forget to pop the&nbsp;widget.

00:02:26.356 --> 00:02:31.163
In which case, you'll accidentally delete it a 
second time when you loop through the vector&nbsp;at the end.

00:02:31.163 --> 00:02:33.438
Deleting a pointer twice is 
undefined behavior.

00:02:33.438 --> 00:02:37.000
So if you do this even once on
 accident&nbsp;anywhere in your program,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.209
the entire program is undefined.

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:40.982
This could result in a segvault,

00:02:40.982 --> 00:02:43.155
garbage data being displayed 
to your user

00:02:43.155 --> 00:02:46.604
or worse a silent wrong answer 
in a calculation.

00:02:46.604 --> 00:02:51.750
The&nbsp;idea behind smartpointers is to 
make ownership of the pointer explicit.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:56.820
An owner of a pointer is an&nbsp;object
 that's responsible for deleting the pointer.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:03.120
So a unique pointer models an object
 that&nbsp;has exactly one owner at any given time.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.491
You could transfer or give 
up ownership.

00:03:05.491 --> 00:03:10.882
But when&nbsp;a unique pointer is destroyed,
 it's responsible for deleting whatever it owns.

00:03:10.882 --> 00:03:16.585
This completely gets&nbsp;rid of any confusion about 
when where or who's in charge of deleting a pointer.

00:03:16.585 --> 00:03:19.825
The way we clean&nbsp;up this example
 is by using a vector of unique pointers

00:03:19.825 --> 00:03:22.700
instead of a vector of raw pointers.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.716
So, here we're using a unique pointer
 to widget instead of a pointer to widget.

00:03:26.716 --> 00:03:29.536
Here you can push&nbsp;back unique
 pointers instead of raw pointers.

00:03:29.536 --> 00:03:32.994
You can construct a unique 
pointer yourself passing&nbsp;in a new widget.

00:03:32.994 --> 00:03:35.044
But for reasons beyond the 
scope of this video,

00:03:35.044 --> 00:03:38.634
the factory function make 
unique&nbsp;is a better alternative.

00:03:38.634 --> 00:03:40.975
All make unique does is make 
a unique pointer

00:03:40.975 --> 00:03:44.700
by calling new widget and&nbsp;forwarding all 
the arguments that you passed in.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:50.680
For type safety reasons unique pointer will not&nbsp;
automatically convert to a function expecting a&nbsp;pointer.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.115
Instead you can use the get function to&nbsp;
access the underlying pointer.

00:03:54.115 --> 00:03:57.329
Alternatively if the code that you've
 already written was using&nbsp;references,

00:03:57.329 --> 00:04:00.390
the dereference operator works 
with smart pointers.

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:04.232
Dereferencing the smart pointer 
will&nbsp;dereference its underlying pointer.

00:04:04.232 --> 00:04:08.080
So, if you're passing everything by reference 
and you weren't&nbsp;using pointers in the first place,

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.087
Then your calling code doesn't 
need to change at all.

00:04:11.087 --> 00:04:15.964
In any&nbsp;case, whether using references or pointers, 
this is the recommended thing to do.

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:20.276
It's not recommended&nbsp;to rewrite functions to 
take unique pointers instead of pointers.

00:04:20.276 --> 00:04:23.571
Only the code that dealt&nbsp;with 
creating new widgets or deleting widgets

00:04:23.571 --> 00:04:26.314
would need to be changed 
to use smart pointers.

00:04:26.314 --> 00:04:31.259
All the code in the middle that just uses
 widget pointers or references shouldn't need to change.

00:04:31.259 --> 00:04:32.770
Then we get to another benefit.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:37.054
We no longer have to remember to delete this 
widget before popping&nbsp;it off the back.

00:04:37.054 --> 00:04:40.273
Popping off a unique pointer 
will automatically delete the resource.

00:04:40.273 --> 00:04:44.629
In the second&nbsp;case, the compiler automatically 
stops us from making a potential mistake.

00:04:44.629 --> 00:04:49.827
The compiler now forces&nbsp;us to move into 
this local variable last instead of copying into it.

00:04:49.827 --> 00:04:50.976
This might seem a bit annoying.

00:04:50.976 --> 00:04:56.414
But it actually protects you from the possibility of double
 deleting like we had in the raw pointer&nbsp;situation.

00:04:56.414 --> 00:05:00.002
You can use the arrow operator on a
 new pointer just like you would a normal pointer.

00:05:00.002 --> 00:05:02.917
And finally we don't need to do 
any of these deletes.

00:05:02.917 --> 00:05:05.044
The unique pointers handle 
that for us.

00:05:05.044 --> 00:05:08.149
So&nbsp;overall, the code got 
a little bit shorter.

00:05:08.149 --> 00:05:12.382
But the main benefit is that we just don't 
have to worry&nbsp;about new's and delete's anymore.

00:05:12.382 --> 00:05:15.633
Why burden yourself with that 
when the compiler can do it for you?

00:05:15.633 --> 00:05:18.960
And&nbsp;this is where a lot 
of other tutorials might end.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.182
You see some flashy code fixing one
 example&nbsp;and a specific case

00:05:23.182 --> 00:05:27.433
Someone that you don't really know tells you 
to use this thing&nbsp;that you don't really understand.

00:05:27.433 --> 00:05:28.885
But maybe you're super vigilant.

00:05:28.885 --> 00:05:33.900
And you at least try to&nbsp;look at 
the source code and see how it works.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.334
So you go to definition.

00:05:36.334 --> 00:05:42.249
And you see this.

00:05:42.249 --> 00:05:45.331
And then you never use 
unique pointer.

00:05:45.331 --> 00:05:50.034
Standard&nbsp;library implementers have 
to write it this way for reasons.

00:05:50.034 --> 00:05:53.940
Reasons that unfortunately&nbsp;force 
the code to be completely unreadable.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:59.040
But the main idea is behind unique pointer are&nbsp;
very simple. And you could write it yourself.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.880
So let's do it. 
Let's write unique pointer.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.390
We'll start with the class template.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:08.616
For now just&nbsp;assume that `T` is 
a normal non-array non-reference type.

00:06:08.616 --> 00:06:10.288
Think an int or a widget.

00:06:10.288 --> 00:06:13.323
The real standard&nbsp;unique pointer has 
a specialization for arrays.

00:06:13.323 --> 00:06:16.772
And it also allows you to specify 
a custom deleter&nbsp;type.

00:06:16.772 --> 00:06:20.275
But we're just going to keep it simple.
 No arrays and just using the built-in delete.

00:06:20.275 --> 00:06:23.545
Maybe&nbsp;we can add custom deleters 
in a follow-up video.

00:06:23.545 --> 00:06:27.290
A unique pointer just wraps a pointer.
 So let's&nbsp;give it a pointer member.

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:31.075
The main feature of unique pointer 
is that it calls delete and it's&nbsp;destructor.

00:06:31.075 --> 00:06:34.684
By putting delete in the destructor,
 we ensure that the pointer is eventually deleted.

00:06:34.684 --> 00:06:39.574
Because C++ guarantees that destructors 
are called even if there's an exception.

00:06:39.574 --> 00:06:41.959
Technically speaking, it's okay 
to delete an null pointer

00:06:41.959 --> 00:06:44.798
which is going to be our 
not-holding-anything value.

00:06:44.798 --> 00:06:47.141
But I'm setting us up for 
custom deleters in the future.

00:06:47.141 --> 00:06:49.957
And a custom deleter might&nbsp;
not support that.

00:06:49.957 --> 00:06:52.380
That's why we have 
this if check.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:56.773
Next, we need constructors. 
The real unique pointer&nbsp;has a ton of them.

00:06:56.773 --> 00:06:59.144
But you really only need 
just a few.

00:06:59.144 --> 00:07:04.080
We'll have one to construct from a raw pointer&nbsp;that 
just initializes our pointer to that value.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:07.291
And a default constructor to 
hold a null pointer.

00:07:07.291 --> 00:07:11.714
Now, it's common to have a copy constructor and a 
copy assignment operator to go along with it

00:07:11.714 --> 00:07:13.486
that make copies of 
your object.

00:07:13.486 --> 00:07:16.635
But for a unique pointer that 
doesn't make sense.

00:07:16.635 --> 00:07:18.165
If we made a&nbsp;
copy of a unique pointer,

00:07:18.165 --> 00:07:21.519
then we'd have two unique pointers
 that both think they own the&nbsp;same object.

00:07:21.519 --> 00:07:23.880
And would both try 
to delete it.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:24.925
That would be incorrect.

00:07:24.925 --> 00:07:29.188
Unique ownership implies&nbsp;that copying
 a unique pointer would be a bug.

00:07:29.188 --> 00:07:34.200
So in this case, we explicitly delete the&nbsp;
copy constructor and copy assignment operator.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:39.083
Next let's define functions for either
 giving up&nbsp;or changing ownership.

00:07:39.083 --> 00:07:42.958
First up is release
 which is for giving up ownership.

00:07:42.958 --> 00:07:47.100
We've set our internal&nbsp;pointer 
to null and then return the old one.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:48.623
We don't delete the old one.

00:07:48.623 --> 00:07:53.661
Giving up&nbsp;ownership means that the caller is taking 
that responsibility it's their problem now.

00:07:53.661 --> 00:07:57.725
Also&nbsp;note that this pattern of setting a
 value and returning the old one is extremely common.

00:07:57.725 --> 00:08:00.960
And it&nbsp;can be written this 
way using standard exchange.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.455
Reset on the other hand swaps 
out the thing&nbsp;that we own.

00:08:04.455 --> 00:08:08.689
There's no one else that's taking 
that responsibility we still own the old one.

00:08:08.689 --> 00:08:13.149
So after we take ownership of the new pointer, 
we need to delete the old one.

00:08:13.149 --> 00:08:17.580
And once again&nbsp;this could be 
written using standard exchange.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:21.723
So, release and reset are kind of the building&nbsp;
blocks that we use to change ownership.

00:08:21.723 --> 00:08:26.760
But so far, we don't have an easy way to directly transfer&nbsp;
ownership from one unique pointer to another.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:30.768
That's what the move constructor 
and move&nbsp;assignment operator are for.

00:08:30.768 --> 00:08:36.077
These operators are characterized by the fact that 
their function&nbsp;argument is an R value reference.

00:08:36.077 --> 00:08:41.019
I'm definitely not going to attempt to explain value 
categories&nbsp;in the middle of another video.

00:08:41.019 --> 00:08:44.195
Suffice it to say that our value
 references are typically&nbsp;temporaries

00:08:44.195 --> 00:08:47.100
or otherwise about 
to be destroyed.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.773
Think of a function's return value

00:08:48.773 --> 00:08:53.100
or something&nbsp;explicitly cast 
to R value using standard move.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:58.560
The whole purpose of this is to allow you to 
steal&nbsp;the guts of another object before it's destroyed.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.774
In our case, stealing the guts have
 another unique&nbsp;pointer

00:09:00.774 --> 00:09:03.676
means asking it to release 
ownership of its held pointer.

00:09:03.676 --> 00:09:06.403
And then assigning that 
to&nbsp;ourselves.

00:09:06.403 --> 00:09:08.349
Move assignment is similar.

00:09:08.349 --> 00:09:11.206
We guard against assigning 
to ourselves.

00:09:11.206 --> 00:09:16.243
Ask the other thing&nbsp;to release ownership 
and then take ownership for ourselves using reset.

00:09:16.243 --> 00:09:18.907
We need to use reset here&nbsp;
instead of just assigning the pointer

00:09:18.907 --> 00:09:20.368
because we might already 
own something

00:09:20.368 --> 00:09:24.360
that needs to be&nbsp;deleted before 
we take ownership of something new.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:25.658
And that's basically it.

00:09:25.658 --> 00:09:27.364
We just need to define&nbsp;some getters

00:09:27.364 --> 00:09:31.796
and other convenience functions to 
make this usable like a normal pointer.

00:09:31.796 --> 00:09:37.010
Operator bool allows us to do if checks
 and other boolean-like things on the pointer.

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:39.140
The get&nbsp;function just gives out 
the underlying pointer

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:42.063
so that people that don't care about
 ownership can&nbsp;just use it.

00:09:42.063 --> 00:09:47.301
Operator Arrow allows us to forward 
Arrow operations down to the underlying pointer.

00:09:47.301 --> 00:09:52.056
So&nbsp;that p arrow something will 
result in endpointer arrow something.

00:09:52.056 --> 00:09:56.460
And finally there's operator&nbsp;
star or the dereference operator if you prefer.

00:09:56.460 --> 00:10:01.740
It allows star p on the unique pointer&nbsp;
to end up dereferencing the held pointer.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:06.660
Zooming out. The whole thing fit 
in&nbsp;under 50 lines of pretty easy code.&nbsp;

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:10.680
And if you ignore blank lines, I&nbsp;
can even sneak in make unique too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:14.735
Okay, I'll zoom in on that for a sec but
 this is&nbsp;just icing on the cake.

00:10:14.735 --> 00:10:17.208
Just change standard unique 
pointer to unique pointer

00:10:17.208 --> 00:10:19.690
and standard make unique&nbsp;
to R make unique.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:21.840
And the whole thing 
works as is.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:27.060
It doesn't matter if an exception is thrown. Our&nbsp;used 
widget function didn't have to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:30.066
We don't have to remember 
to manually delete&nbsp;anything.

00:10:30.066 --> 00:10:34.109
If we tried to break unique ownership
 by copying, the compiler would stop us.

00:10:34.109 --> 00:10:36.120
I mean, it's not&nbsp;like 
a great error message.

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:39.714
C++ could definitely use some
 improvement on that front.

00:10:39.714 --> 00:10:41.244
But it does&nbsp;stop us.

00:10:41.244 --> 00:10:45.235
It forces us to use move in which 
case ownership is transferred gracefully.

00:10:45.235 --> 00:10:47.608
And we don't&nbsp;have to loop over the vector and clean up at the end.

00:10:47.608 --> 00:10:50.015
All that's just taken care of.

00:10:50.015 --> 00:10:52.911
Now of course,&nbsp;smart pointers 
are not a silver bullet.

00:10:52.911 --> 00:10:55.520
They are but one tool 
in your bag.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:58.429
It's possible that unique&nbsp;
ownership does not fit your problem.

00:10:58.429 --> 00:11:02.742
Although maybe shared ownership 
and a shared pointer would be a&nbsp;better fit.

00:11:02.742 --> 00:11:04.335
But even then maybe not.

00:11:04.336 --> 00:11:08.050
It's possible to accidentally try 
to use a move from object.

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:10.581
And&nbsp;it's possible that one of 
your functions tries to keep a reference

00:11:10.581 --> 00:11:14.460
that becomes a dangling reference
&nbsp; once the unique pointer goes out of scope.

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:19.355
So using a unique pointer doesn't automatically&nbsp;
protect you or make your code correct.

00:11:19.355 --> 00:11:22.271
However, the benefit 
of smart pointers is clear.

00:11:22.271 --> 00:11:25.380
Smart pointers&nbsp;reduce the 
number of bugs that you write.

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:29.544
And unless you have a good reason
 not to and there&nbsp;are some good reasons.

00:11:29.544 --> 00:11:32.405
Then you probably should be 
using them.

00:11:32.405 --> 00:11:33.607
As always thanks for watching.

